<h2 id="why">Why reverse-engineer</h2>
<p>For every stage, some published method already solves part of the problem: a Bayesian-network compiler, Bell-test
entropy accounting, quantum causal-state models, Pixel2Cancer, scanner fingerprinting, VICTRE and others. Rebuilding
each method ourselves, from the paper and without copying proprietary code, gives us components we understand, can
cite, and can adapt to lungs. Every target goes through the same lifecycle:</p>
<ol class="steps">
  <li><strong>Dissect</strong>: a dossier with method, equations, parameters and claimed result.</li>
  <li><strong>Reproduce</strong>: match one published number or figure in <code>repro/</code> before changing anything.</li>
  <li><strong>Adapt</strong>: to lungs, 3-D, certification and our contracts.</li>
  <li><strong>Integrate</strong>, or <strong>reject</strong> with a written reason.</li>
</ol>
<p>States move <code>listed → dissected → reproduced → adapted → integrated | rejected</code>, recorded per letter in
<code>&lt;L&gt;_&lt;name&gt;/status.json</code> and shown as badges in the <a href="#targets">target table</a>.</p>

<h2 id="privacy">Privacy by construction</h2>
<p>No patient voxels enter any generator. Host CT scans (LIDC-IDRI, later Mayo Siemens raw data) are only
<em>read</em>: to choose a site, measure the scanner fingerprint, and insert into. The tumor's appearance comes from
<strong>fitted parameters</strong> (shape, edge profile, HU per nodule type) and published statistics only. Learned
refiners trained on patients (DiffTumor, MAISI) appear only as ablations.</p>
